// The story a session tells about a fault: one report as its own timeline holds
// it, the whole story as it goes out, and the maneuver it is running while it
// tells it.
//
// The entry and the maneuver are state the session keeps. A report is raised
// faster than one datagram per execution can carry it away, so the ring of
// entries is what holds the story across the executions that append to it; and a
// wind-down runs across executions too, on one clock opened once.
//
// The story is what leaves: one message carrying every row the session has
// narrated, republished whole whenever a row is added, so a reader that arrives
// late has the whole of it from the first message it sees.
//
// They sit together because they are the two halves of the same episode -- what
// is being done about a fault, and what has been said about it -- and because a
// consumer of one is a consumer of the other. How far a maneuver got is here for
// the same reason: the wind-down core writes it and the narration carries it as
// one of a report's numbers. None of the three is the fault vocabulary itself: a
// matcher on a raise imports `motion/faults.clk` and rebuilds for nothing that
// changes here.

#![generate(cpp)]
#![cpp(namespace=brenn_reachy::motion)]

use motion::faults;
use motion::reports;

// One report, as the session's own timeline holds it before it goes out.
//
// The same five values the wire's `Report` carries, in the fields a state schema
// holds them in. It stands here because the session keeps its story in a ring of
// these and publishes the whole of it, so one row has to be writable into a slot
// and into a message alike. The ring is bounded because a story is narration --
// the classification that mattered already travelled on the channel that raised
// it.
schema TimelineEntry
{
    uuid: 22a9d649-3287-41af-8b4b-98ca9efe34d9;
    fields
    {
        // When it happened -- the instant the report is about, not the execution
        // that will publish it.
        #0 time: SyncTime;

        // What it is about. `none` is what an unwritten row holds and is no
        // report, which is what makes an empty ring readable.
        #1 kind: reports::ReportKind;

        // The first specific, meaning stated per kind. Zero where the kind names
        // none.
        #2 a: UInt32;

        // The second specific, likewise.
        #3 b: UInt32;

        // The measured specific, likewise. Carried bit for bit, a non-finite
        // number included: a report is narration, and a story dropped for an
        // unreadable measurement loses the part that was readable with it.
        #4 detail: Float64;
    }
}

// The whole story so far: every report the session has narrated, oldest first.
//
// Cumulative rather than one row per message. The reader this crosses to is a
// log, and a logger attaches to a channel whenever it happens to start -- so a
// row published as its own message before that instant is a row nobody can read
// afterwards. A message that carries the whole story has no head to lose: the
// newest one is the account of the run, whichever copy a reader saw first.
//
// Sixty-four rows, which is the ring the session keeps them in, not a multiple
// of it: this capacity is what bounds that ring, and a ring grown past it would
// have rows with nowhere to go. The two lengths are pinned against each other by
// a case beside the ring. A story longer than the ring loses its oldest rows and
// says how many in `dropped`, which is the one thing a reader cannot work out
// from the rows it holds.
schema Timeline
{
    uuid: 14b5f331-e3c2-4051-a6bd-6dbc47adb3ff;
    fields
    {
        // The rows, oldest first. Every one of them narrates something: a story
        // never carries the zero kind an unwritten slot row holds.
        #0 entries: VarArray<TimelineEntry, 64>;

        // How many rows were dropped off the front to make room, all run. A
        // rise says narration was lost, never a record: what mattered about a
        // fault travelled on the channel that raised it.
        #1 dropped: UInt32;
    }
}

// The wind-down maneuver the session is running, or that it is running none.
//
// Two facts and the bookkeeping around them: which ending the machine is being
// judged by, and when the one clock is spent. The clock is opened once, when the
// maneuver begins, and a servo dropping out mid-stow re-commands the stow from
// where the machine stands without ever re-opening it -- which is why the
// deadline is here rather than derived from a budget and a start.
schema WindDownSnap
{
    uuid: 898a32a4-45cd-41b7-8b1b-b7d7e5a477b2;
    fields
    {
        // Whether a maneuver is running. Every field below means nothing when
        // this is false.
        #0 active: Bool;

        // The ending the machine is judged by -- `class` is what this is called
        // everywhere else in the stack, and is a keyword in the language this
        // schema is compiled into. The sticky maximum of the ending the maneuver
        // began with and every one raised since, so it only ever asks more.
        #1 ending: faults::EndingKind;

        // When the one clock is spent.
        #2 deadline: SyncTime;

        // The `epoch` of the schedule the stow was last commanded with. The
        // whole of what tells a stow already asked for from one still to be
        // published: the host writes it as it publishes a stow, and a wake
        // whose schedule is still under that epoch asks for nothing. A
        // maneuver that owes a stow -- one just opened, or one a condition
        // re-ranked -- records the epoch before the schedule's, so the two
        // cannot read as agreeing. The core that runs the maneuver never reads
        // it: what to command is its answer, and whether the command has
        // already gone out is the host's bookkeeping.
        #3 commanded_epoch: UInt32;
    }
}

// How far a wind-down got, in the three shapes it can end in.
//
// Narrower than the response vocabulary, because a wind-down that has begun
// ends: the question left is whether the machine was measured where the maneuver
// was to leave it, reached the end unmeasured, or was given up on.
//
// It stands here rather than with the report kinds because the wind-down core is
// what writes it and the narration is what reads it, which is exactly the pair
// this module already holds. The number is what a `winddown_outcome` report's
// first specific carries, so it is append-only: a value inserted among these
// would move the family under a reader that had already recorded one.
enum WindDownOutcome
{
    uuid: 5d84b0c1-9e32-4a77-b6f0-1c3a7e9d2b48;
    options
    {
        underlying_type: UInt8;
    }
    values
    {
        // No outcome. What a slot that has concluded no maneuver holds, and no
        // conclusion the core ever reaches.
        #0 none default { underlying_value: 0; }
        // The stow reached its end and the machine was measured there.
        #1 completed { underlying_value: 1; }
        // The maneuver reached its own end with nothing confirming it landed --
        // every joint that carries the head went limp before the stow could put
        // it down, so nothing commanded the descent and nothing measured where it
        // stopped.
        #2 unconfirmed { underlying_value: 2; }
        // The maneuver was given up on and the immediate release took over: the
        // one clock ran out, or the stow itself ended on something the machine
        // did.
        #3 fell_through { underlying_value: 3; }
    }
}
